WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Why filters alone fail.

2
00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:12.867
The first instinct is to detect the attack: a classifier on incoming content, a regex for “ignore previous instructions,” a second model asked “is this an injection?

3
00:00:13.147 --> 00:00:15.195
” These help, and vendors use them.

4
00:00:15.475 --> 00:00:29.021
Anthropic scans untrusted content entering the context with classifiers, Google describes purpose-built detection models for Gemini in Workspace, and OpenAI’s agent guide recommends input guardrails.

5
00:00:29.301 --> 00:00:35.979
But all three vendors present them as one layer, not as the defense, and the reasons are worth stating precisely.

6
00:00:36.259 --> 00:00:39.821
First, detectors are probabilistic, and you face volume.

7
00:00:40.101 --> 00:00:57.531
Anthropic’s November 2025 research on browser agents reported reducing attack success to about 1% with its newest model and safeguards, and said plainly that 1% still represents meaningful risk and that no browser agent is immune.

8
00:00:57.811 --> 00:01:02.973
A small miss rate times every document your agent reads is a steady stream of misses.

9
00:01:03.253 --> 00:01:04.853
Second, attackers adapt.

10
00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:07.821
Benchmark rates are measured against known attacks.

11
00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:18.320
Anthropic’s May 2026 containment post reports that, on one red-teaming benchmark, a recent Claude model held attack success to roughly 0.

12
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:24.744
1% on a single attempt and around 5 to 6% after 100 adaptive attempts.

13
00:01:25.024 --> 00:01:28.309
The attacker gets to retry; you have to win every time.

14
00:01:28.589 --> 00:01:32.493
Third, detection judges content, and content can be disguised.

15
00:01:32.773 --> 00:01:39.963
Encode the data in base64, split an instruction across two issues, phrase it as a policy notice from an admin.

16
00:01:40.243 --> 00:01:46.173
A content filter sees text; it cannot see that the recipient address came from an untrusted issue.

17
00:01:46.453 --> 00:01:50.507
The conclusion is not that model-level robustness is worthless.

18
00:01:50.787 --> 00:02:03.267
It is valuable, it improves with each model generation, and it differs between models, which makes it a legitimate input to model selection (measure it on your own injection suite, as in this week’s lab).

19
00:02:03.547 --> 00:02:11.120
The conclusion is that robustness lowers how often the agent tries something bad, while only design limits what happens when it does.
